<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Bank</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The West Bank is the territory west of the Jordan River that was part of Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. In the 1948 war Jordan's Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> held it together with East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and Jordan annexed both in 1950; Israel occupied the territory in the war of 1967, and its status remains at the centre of the Israeli–Palestinian conflict.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-bank.jpg" alt="West Bank" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Bank_in_Palestine_(%2Bclaimed).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The West Bank is a landlocked territory on the western bank of the Jordan River, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. It is bordered by Jordan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> to the east and by Israel along the Green Line to the south, west and north. It has a land area of about 5,640 square kilometres and mostly rugged upland. Its elevation runs from the shore of the Dead Sea, 408 metres below sea level, to Mount Nabi Yunis at 1,030 metres. The name West Bank emerged for the territory occupied in 1948 and annexed in 1950 by Jordan, to distinguish it from Jordanian land east of the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1517 to 1917 the area was under Ottoman rule, as part of Ottoman Syria. In the early Ottoman period much of the northern West Bank formed part of the Turabay Emirate, a semi-autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> polity centred at Lajjun. Turkey renounced its claims in 1923, and the area became part of the British Mandate for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. In 1947 the UN General Assembly recommended that it become part of a future Arab state, a proposal the Arab states opposed.</p><p>In the 1948 war the 1949 Armistice Agreements fixed the interim boundary between Israel and Jordan. After the Jericho Conference of December 1948, Transjordan annexed the area west of the river in 1950. Palestinian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> there were granted Jordanian citizenship and half the seats in the Jordanian parliament, and only the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> recognised the annexation formally.</p><p>In June 1967 Israel captured the West Bank and East Jerusalem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>. Apart from East Jerusalem, the territory was not annexed, and it remained under military control until 1982. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> summit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> in 1974 named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> the sole legitimate representative of the Palestinian people. Jordan did not formally relinquish its claim until 1988. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo-accords">Oslo Accords</a> of 1993 left the final status of the territory to later negotiation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Bank” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its outcome shaped the region: the State of Israel, the <b>West Bank</b> annexed by Transjordan, Gaza under Egypt, and the 1948 Palestinian expulsion and flight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Six-Day War of 1967 religious Zionists came to see settling the <b>West Bank</b> as a commandment of God, and religious Zionist ideology motivated the assassination of Yitzhak Rabin in 1995.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ownership is contested: the scrolls were mostly found after the Jordanian annexation of the <b>West Bank</b> and came under Israeli control after the 1967 war, and Jordan and the Palestinian Authority dispute Israel's custody, while Israel's claim rests on historical and religious grounds.</blockquote><a href="/work/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Fighting ended with Israel holding Sinai, Gaza, the <b>West Bank</b>, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-10">10 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In six days Israel took Sinai, Gaza, the <b>West Bank</b>, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-5">5 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1948 war the Arab Legion held the <b>West Bank</b> and East Jerusalem, which Jordan annexed in 1950.</blockquote><a href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan</a></li></ul></section>
